# One box or two? Newcomb's problem
tags: ["decision theory","causal decision theory","evidential decision theory","dominance"]
teaches: ["concepts/newcombs-problem","concepts/causal-vs-evidential-decision-theory","concepts/dominance-reasoning"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed","b":"gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace","take":"lines"}
prompt: "Why do two airtight arguments recommend opposite choices? What does the split between one-boxers and two-boxers say about how we decide?"
next: {"title":"Can any voting rule be fair? Arrow's impossibility theorem","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/paradoxes/12-can-any-voting-rule-be-fair-arrow-s.cast"}

## Newcomb's problem
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: The Predictor saw that coming.
    good: A respectable haul from both boxes.
    poor: The Predictor kept the million this time.
    none: 'Fair enough: you took neither box.'
sources:
    - id: nozick1969
      title: Newcomb's Problem and Two Principles of Choice
      authors: Robert Nozick
      year: 1969
      finding: First published statement of the problem, credited to the physicist William Newcomb; Nozick reports that to almost everyone the answer seems perfectly clear and obvious, yet people divide almost evenly (in Essays in Honor of Carl G. Hempel, ed. N. Rescher).
    - id: lewis1981
      title: Why Ain'cha Rich?
      authors: David Lewis
      year: 1981
      finding: 'A two-boxer''s reply to the one-boxers'' taunt: the predictor rewards those disposed to one-box, so the million was never on offer to a two-boxer (Noûs 15: 377–380).'
    - id: philpapers2020
      title: 'Philosophers on Philosophy: The 2020 PhilPapers Survey'
      authors: David Bourget and David J. Chalmers
      year: 2023
      finding: 'Newcomb''s problem, 1,071 philosophers: 31 % one box, 39 % two boxes; decision theorists (48): 21 % one box, 73 % two boxes.'
    - id: qt1984
      title: 'Causal versus diagnostic contingencies: On self-deception and on the voter''s illusion'
      authors: George A. Quattrone and Amos Tversky
      year: 1984
      finding: 'Students told that a long life goes with more (or less) tolerance of cold water after exercise shifted their tolerance on the second trial towards the long-life sign (J. Personality and Social Psychology 46: 237–248).'
      doi: 10.1037/0022-3514.46.2.237

